IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: W.P. (C) No. 494 of 2012
CITATION: (2017) 10 SCC 1
YEAR: 2017

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: J.S. Khehar, J. Chelameswar, S.A. Bobde, D.Y. Chandrachud, et al.

PARTIES:
Justice K.S. Puttaswamy vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Article 21, Article 14, Article 19

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
right to privacy, informational privacy, surveillance, data protection, three fold test

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Nine-judge constitution bench unanimously held that privacy is a fundamental right emanating from Article 21, establishing the requirements of legality, legitimate aim, and proportionality for state infringement.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Article 21, Article 14, Article 19.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

